{
  "title": "Investment Calculator with Increasing Contributions",
  "metaDescription": "Free investment calculator with increasing contributions. Add a yearly increase to your monthly deposit and see how much faster your balance grows over time.",
  "targetKeyword": "investment calculator with increasing contributions",
  "h1": "Investment Calculator with Increasing Contributions",
  "introText": "Raising your monthly contribution a little each year makes your investments grow faster than flat deposits, and the gap widens the longer you keep investing. With a $10,000 starting balance, $500 a month, and a 7% annual return, a 3% yearly raise turns a flat-deposit balance of $300,850.72 into $367,509.35 after 20 years. This investment calculator with increasing contributions runs the same math on your own numbers.\n\nIn the calculator above, enter your starting balance, your monthly contribution for year 1, your annual return, the number of years, and the yearly increase in contribution as a percent. You'll see your future value, the total you put in (starting balance included), and the investment growth earned on top.\n\nA note under the results shows your monthly deposit in the final year and how much the yearly increase adds compared with keeping the deposit flat. Below that, a bar splits your contributions from your growth, and a table lists the balance year by year. The projection uses one fixed return every year and leaves out inflation, taxes, fees, and contribution limits.",
  "howItWorks": "The calculator above raises your monthly deposit by your chosen percent at the start of each new year and keeps it flat for all 12 months within that year. Year 1 uses the amount you enter, year 2 uses amount x (1 + rate), and year 3 uses amount x (1 + rate)^2. The pattern repeats through the final year of your timeline.\n\nReturns compound monthly at your annual return divided by 12. Each month, the calculator applies one rule: balance = balance x (1 + annual return / 12) + that month's deposit. At a 7% return, the monthly rate is 7% / 12, or about 0.583%.\n\nThat monthly rule is compound interest, which the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) site [Investor.gov](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/glossary/compound-interest) defines as interest earned on both the original money and the interest already earned. Each month's growth joins the balance, and the larger balance earns the next month's return.\n\nAt a 3% yearly raise from $500, the deposit is $500.00 a month in year 1, $515.00 in year 2 ($500 x 1.03), and $530.45 in year 3 ($500 x 1.03 squared). The steps keep going until the deposit reaches $876.75 a month in year 20.\n\nA yearly raise changes nothing in year 1, because both plans deposit the same $500 a month. On the base case, the flat plan and the 3% plan both end year 1 at $16,919.19. After that the balances split: $52,029.56 versus $49,972.70 at year 5, $117,640.78 versus $106,639.02 at year 10, and $217,642.23 versus $186,970.62 at year 15.\n\nThe gap between the two plans grows each period, from $2,056.86 at year 5 to $11,001.76 at year 10, $30,671.61 at year 15, and $66,658.63 at year 20. Most of the 20-year gap is your own extra money. The 3% plan puts in $171,222.25 against $130,000.00 for the flat plan, so $41,222.25 of the gap is deposits and the other $25,436.38 is extra growth.\n\nA longer timeline widens the gap between a 3% raise and flat deposits. At the same inputs, 10 years gives $117,640.78 with the raise versus $106,639.02 flat, and 30 years gives $914,744.96 versus $691,150.47. That is an $11,001.76 difference at 10 years and a $223,594.49 difference at 30 years.\n\nOver 10 years, the 3% plan puts in $78,783.28 against $70,000.00 flat ($10,000 plus $500 for 120 months), so $8,783.28 of the $11,001.76 gap is your own deposits. Over 30 years, it puts in $295,452.49 against $190,000.00 flat ($10,000 plus $500 for 360 months). That leaves $118,142.00 of the $223,594.49 gap as extra growth, more than the $105,452.49 of extra deposits.\n\nThe deposit itself climbs further over a longer run: $652.39 a month by year 10, $876.75 by year 20, and $1,178.28 by year 30. Enter the full number of years you plan to invest, because cutting the timeline short removes the years where the gap grows fastest.\n\nChoose a raise percent you can fund every year, because the calculator applies it from year 2 through the last year without a break. A raise you plan to make, such as sending part of each expected pay raise to your account, gives a more usable projection than a percent you hope to reach someday. Read the final-year deposit in the note: at a 10% raise, the base case deposit climbs from $500 to $3,057.95 a month by year 20, so lower the percent if that amount is out of reach.\n\nThe calculator does not check contribution limits, so it keeps raising the deposit past any annual cap your account has. This page does not publish current limits for any account type. If a cap applies, lower the raise percent until the final-year monthly deposit in the note, times 12, stays at or below that cap, since the final year holds the largest deposit.\n\nFor retirement account projections, this site also has a [401k calculator](/retirement/401k-calculator/) and a [retirement savings calculator](/retirement/retirement-savings-calculator/).\n\nThe calculator does not adjust for inflation, so the future value is stated in future dollars. To see the result in today's buying power, enter a return after inflation in the annual return box instead of the raw return. The Financial Industry Regulatory Authority (FINRA) covers historical asset-class returns and inflation on its [risk page](https://www.finra.org/investors/investing/investing-basics/risk).\n\nThe return you enter can move the result by more than the raise does. In the base case, dropping the return from 7% to 5% cuts the 3%-raise balance from $367,509.35 to $290,125.95, a drop of $77,383.40. That drop is bigger than the $66,658.63 the 3% raise added, so the raise plan at 5% ends below the flat plan at 7%.\n\nA yearly step-up is a variation on [dollar-cost averaging](https://www.finra.org/investors/investing/investing-basics/dollar-cost-averaging), which FINRA defines as investing the same amount on a fixed schedule regardless of price. With a step-up, the amount rises on a schedule each year. If you plan to keep one fixed amount, use the [dollar-cost averaging calculator](/investing/dollar-cost-averaging-calculator/).\n\nEnter your own starting balance and year 1 deposit in the investment calculator with increasing contributions above, then read the note to see how much your planned yearly raise adds over a flat deposit. Run it a second time at a lower return to see how much of that gain depends on the rate you assumed.",
  "commonMistakes": [
    "Expecting the raise to show up right away. Year 1 deposits match a flat plan, so both base-case balances read $16,919.19 after year 1, and the difference builds from year 2 onward.",
    "Picking a raise percent you cannot keep up. The calculator applies it every year, and a 10% raise turns $500 a month into $3,057.95 a month by year 20, so read the final-year deposit in the note and lower the percent if it is out of reach.",
    "Planning past an account's contribution limit. The calculator does not check limits, so lower the raise percent until 12 times the final-year deposit fits under the cap.",
    "Reading the future value as today's dollars. The calculator does not adjust for inflation, so enter a return after inflation if you want the result in today's buying power.",
    "Planning around one return. At 5% instead of 7%, the base case with a 3% raise reaches $290,125.95, below the $300,850.72 a flat plan reaches at 7%, so run a lower rate as well."
  ],
  "workedExample": "Take the base case: a $10,000 starting balance, $500 a month in year 1, a 7% annual return compounded monthly, and 20 years. With no yearly increase, the calculator above projects $300,850.72, made of $130,000.00 you put in and $170,850.72 of growth. A 3% yearly increase lifts the deposit to $876.75 a month by year 20 and projects $367,509.35 ($171,222.25 put in, $196,287.10 growth), which is $66,658.63 more than flat. A 5% increase reaches $1,263.48 a month by year 20 and projects $425,518.28 ($208,395.72 put in, $217,122.56 growth), or $124,667.56 more than flat. A 10% increase reaches $3,057.95 a month by year 20 and projects $641,628.79 ($353,650.00 put in, $287,978.79 growth), or $340,778.07 more than flat. In all three raise cases, more of the gain comes from extra deposits than from extra growth: at 10%, $223,650.00 of the $340,778.07 gain is your own money and $117,128.07 is growth. Pick the raise whose final-year deposit you can afford, since you must deposit that amount every month of the last year.",
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "How do I calculate investment growth with contributions that increase every year?",
      "answer": "Enter your starting balance, year 1 monthly contribution, annual return, number of years, and yearly increase percent in the calculator above. It raises the deposit by that percent at the start of each year, compounds returns monthly at the annual return divided by 12, and adds each month's deposit. The results show your future value, the total you put in, and the investment growth."
    },
    {
      "question": "What is the formula for a monthly investment that rises each year?",
      "answer": "Each month, balance = balance x (1 + annual return / 12) + that month's deposit. The deposit in year n is the year 1 amount x (1 + raise)^(n - 1), so a $500 deposit with a 3% raise is $515.00 in year 2 and $530.45 in year 3. Repeat the monthly step for every month of the timeline to get the future value."
    },
    {
      "question": "How much does a 3% yearly increase in contributions add?",
      "answer": "On the base case of $10,000, $500 a month, 7%, and 20 years, a 3% yearly increase adds $66,658.63, raising the balance from $300,850.72 to $367,509.35. Starting from $0 with $300 a month for 30 years at 7%, it adds $134,156.69, raising the balance from $365,991.30 to $500,147.99. In that second case you put in $171,271.50 instead of $108,000.00, and the deposit reaches $706.97 a month by year 30."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can I use this as a compound interest calculator with increasing contributions?",
      "answer": "Yes. The calculator above compounds returns monthly, so each month's growth earns a return in later months, and it raises your deposit by the yearly percent you enter. Set the yearly increase to 0% for flat deposits, or set the monthly contribution to $0 to project a starting balance on its own."
    },
    {
      "question": "Does this calculator include inflation?",
      "answer": "No. The calculator above shows results in future dollars with no inflation adjustment. To see the result in today's buying power, enter a return after inflation in the annual return box. The yearly increase in contribution is a separate input, so changing it does not change how inflation is handled."
    },
    {
      "question": "What return rate should I use?",
      "answer": "Use a return that fits your investment mix, then run a lower rate beside it to see the downside. The rate moves the result a lot: the base case with a 3% raise projects $367,509.35 at 7% and $290,125.95 at 5%. The calculator applies whatever rate you enter to every year of the timeline."
    },
    {
      "question": "Is the result guaranteed?",
      "answer": "No. The projection uses one fixed return every year, while historical stock returns are not guaranteed and real markets rise and fall, per FINRA. Treat the future value as an estimate. Rerun the calculator each year with your current balance as the new starting balance to keep the projection current."
    },
    {
      "question": "How is this different from the investment growth calculator?",
      "answer": "The [investment growth calculator](/investing/investment-growth-calculator/) uses one flat monthly contribution for the whole timeline, while this page raises the deposit each year by the percent you enter. Set the yearly increase here to 0% and the two match: the base case gives $300,850.72 here and about $300,851 in that page's published example. If your deposit will stay the same every year, the investment growth calculator is the simpler choice, and for compounding math without a yearly raise, use the [compound interest calculator](/investing/compound-interest-calculator/)."
    }
  ],
